,1,1,9,1,1,1,1,1,1,9,1,8,7,8,1,6,9,2,3,6,1,8,7],[1,1,1,1,1,1,9,1,1,1,1,1,1,9,6,1,8,7,8,1,9,3,2,3,6,1,8],[1,1,1,1,1,1,9,1,1,1,1,1,1,9,3,6,1,8,7,8,9,6,3,2,3,6,1],[1,1,1,1,1,1,9,1,1,1,1,1,1,9,2,3,6,1,8,7,9,1,6,3,2,3,6],[1,1,1,1,1,1,9,1,1,1,1,1,1,9,3,2,3,6,1,8,9,8,1,6,3,2,3],[1,1,1,1,1,1,9,1,1,1,1,1,1,9,6,3,2,3,6,1,9,7,8,1,6,3,2]],"output":[[8,2],[2,6],[1,1]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The previous program crashed because no solid panels were found: the `min()` calls received an empty list. Either the `panels` helper is not returning the boxes I expected, or it returns them in a different form. I will not depend on it. Instead I detect the dividers directly.\n\n**Finding the dividers.** A divider is a full row (or full column) made of one single colour. A row inside the solid area cannot qualify, because it always crosses patterned panels too, so it is never uniform. Here is how this plays out in each grid:\n- **Pair 1:** rows and columns of colour 4 at 5, 11, 17 and 23.\n- **Pair 2:** colour 8 at 4, 9, 14, 19 and 24.\n- **Test:** colour 9 at 6, 13 and 20.\n\n**Building the panel lattice.** The bands between consecutive dividers, plus the grid edges, give the rows and columns of the panel lattice. I index panels by their band numbers, not by absolute coordinates. That way the 27×27 test grid works the same as the 29×29 training grids.\n\n**Turning panels into output cells.**\n- A panel counts as \"solid\" if every cell in it has one colour.\n- The patterned panels never pass this check.\n- I take the bounding block of the solid panels in the lattice.\n- Each solid panel becomes one output cell, holding its colour at its lattice position.\n\n**What this gives on each grid:**\n- **Pair 1:** `1 8 / 0 3`.\n- **Pair 2:** `2 2 2 / 3 9 2 / 2 1 2`. Solid panels of colour 2 are kept as normal cells.\n- **Test:** the solid block is lattice rows 1–3 and columns 0–1, so the output is `8 2 / 2 6 / 1 1`.\n\n**Teaching stages.** I emit the output canvas after each lattice row of the block has been filled, then emit the finished grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    # divider lines: full rows/cols of a single colour\n    full_rows = [y for y in range(H) if (g[y] == g[y, 0]).all()]\n    full_cols = [x for x in range(W) if (g[:, x] == g[0, x]).all()]\n    def bands(lines, n):\n        res, start = [], 0\n        for l in sorted(lines) + [n]:\n            if l > start:\n                res.append((start, l - 1))\n            start = l + 1\n        return res\n    rb = bands(full_rows, H)\n    cb = bands(full_cols, W)\n    solid = []\n    for i, (y0, y1) in enumerate(rb):\n        for j, (x0, x1) in enumerate(cb):\n            p = g[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1]\n            if p.size > 1 and (p == p.flat[0]).all():\n                solid.append((i, j, int(p.flat[0])))\n    r0 = min(s[0] for s in solid); r1 = max(s[0] for s in solid)\n    c0 = min(s[1] for s in solid); c1 = max(s[1] for s in solid)\n    out = np.full((r1 - r0 + 1, c1 - c0 + 1), solid[0][2], dtype=int)\n    by_row = {}\n    for r, c, col in solid:\n        by_row.setdefault(r, []).append((c, col))\n    rows = sorted(by_row)\n    for k, r in enumerate(rows):\n        for c, col in by_row[r]:\n            out[r - r0, c - c0] = col\n        if k < len(rows) - 1:\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"Placed the solid panels of lattice row {r} as one output row.\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"All solid panels condensed to one cell each: final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Either the `panels` helper is not returning the boxes I expected, or it returns them in a different form."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Instead I detect the dividers directly."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Finding the dividers.** A divider is a full row (or full column) made of one single colour."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- A panel counts as \"solid\" if every cell in it has one colour."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"4fc7e00581ab52d13c0673412e70ff1fa91789f5e845bb6f613174295606f8ce","task_sha256":"e9075e6d5c643f2eb9b85b5901c68f72ef95169c5f123c0738a2650786b5b21b","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
